    "created_at": "2025-09-08T10:40:23Z",
    "updated_at": "2025-09-08T10:40:23Z",
    "body": "> However, all browsers do not set the CSP header for image documents\n\nI don't think this is totally true. At least in chrome, Content Security Policy seems to be correctly applied to the created image document. It's only the main image itself which is not blocked (the request cannot be blocked because it is performed before the Content Security Policy header is retrieved, and chrome does not request it again after the document has been created).\n\nSince Content Security Policy doesn't control rendering of the image but rather whether the request/response are blocked or not, this behavior makes sense to me. I guess this still does not really adhere to the spec, since the html spec seems to imply that the image src is fetched again (now respecting CSP) after the image document has been created.\n\nI've created [tests](https://chromium-review.googlesource.com/c/chromium/src/+/6920888) for this. Once I get a +1 in chromium, they will be exported to WPT and I'll be able to see if other browsers also follow chrome's behavior here. If so, it could make sense to understand how we can tweak the spec to mandate that behavior.",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
    "reactions": {
